   "subject": "Re: Standardized Residuals for DWLS in lavaan",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Here is a more fleshed out illustration of what I meant.<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#800\"># The Holzinger and Swineford (1939) example</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>HS</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39; visual  =~ x1 + x2 + x3<br>              textual =~ x4 + x5 + x6<br>              speed   =~ x7 + x8 + x9 &#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>fit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lavaan</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">HS</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#606\">HolzingerSwineford1939</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>              </span><span style=\"color:#008\">auto</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">var</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">auto</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">first</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>              </span><span style=\"color:#008\">auto</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">cov</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>summary</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">measures</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>rawResiduals </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> residuals</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> type</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;raw&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>rawResiduals<br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">class</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rawResiduals$cov</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>madeUpSEs </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(.</span><span style=\"color:#066\">01</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ncol</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">9</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nrow</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">9</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># in practice you would bootstrap these</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>rawResiduals$cov </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> madeUpSEs </span><span style=\"color:#800\"># This is how you could compute standardized or normalized residuals.</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>Normalized and standardized residuals are like z scores.  So, if you interpret each one as a separate z test, then you invite alpha inflation.<br><br>I do not know how to point to a specific post but see my January 7 post in response to your thread entitled &quot;Chi-square result for large sample size and normed chi-square&quot;.  that post contains sample code for graphing residuals.</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>Keith<br>------------------------<br>Keith A. Markus<br>John Jay College of Criminal Justice, CUNY<br><a href=\"http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426153953000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1VpdrWJX8pxrwUvUNzKcbv\">http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus</a><br>Frontiers of Test Validity Theory: Measurement, Causation and Meaning.<br><a href=\"http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426153953000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2c2gVLFOc4fchrUtZ5bzoq\">http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/</a><div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\"><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px;white-space:normal\"><pre style=\"font-family:&quot;Ubuntu Mono&quot;;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:14.5455px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-size:10.4pt!important;white-space:pre-wrap!important\"></pre></div></span></div></div></div>"
